// Package port is the gogo peripheral API: a port is a special actor with a
// PID, backed by a platform driver. These stubs exist only so programs
// type-check with the standard Go toolchain; the gogo compiler replaces
// every call with VM instructions, so the bodies are never executed.
//
// The struct types Chunk, Out, Done and Closed have fixed type indices
// 31-34 in every image, Host and Post 42-43 (TYPE_* in
// runtime/vm/opcodes.h); keep their field order in sync with
// compiler.reservedTypes. The types of each device are in its own package
// (gogo/gpio, gogo/kbd, gogo/imu, ...).
//
// Send a command to a port with actor.Send(p, cmd); any actor holding the
// PID may. Events go to the owner, the actor that opened the port, as
// ordinary messages under its mailbox quota. The owner's exit closes its
// ports; actor.Kill on a port PID closes it. Ports cannot be linked or
// monitored. The owner may give a port to another actor with Handoff.
//
// The stream protocol. Every port that moves bytes (tcp, tls, imu, ir,
// mic, speaker, screen) speaks the same four messages: Chunk brings input,
// Out sends output and Done answers each Out, and Closed says the port
// ended by itself. The events carry From, the port's PID, so one actor can
// own several ports and tell their events apart.
package port

import (
	"gogo/actor"
	"gogo/bytes"
)

// Chunk is a stream event: the next block of input. From is the port. Seq
// counts the chunks the port made, from 1, dropped ones included, so a gap
// in Seq means chunks were lost; TimeMs is when the data began (the low 31
// bits of the millisecond clock; each port documents its precision);
// Dropped is how many chunks were dropped since the last one delivered (a
// chunk over the owner's mailbox quota is dropped and counted; the owner
// is not killed). A port that loses nothing (tcp, tls: the peer resends
// instead) always says 0.
type Chunk struct {
	From                 actor.PID
	Seq, TimeMs, Dropped int
	Data                 bytes.Bytes
}

// Out is a stream command: send Data. At most 2 Outs are in flight per port
// (one running, one queued); a third is refused with Done{Seq: -1} and the
// port carries on.
type Out struct{ Data bytes.Bytes }

// Done is a stream event: an Out has finished. From is the port. Every
// accepted Out gets one, in the order the Outs were sent, and an Out counts
// as in flight until its Done is delivered. A refused Out is answered at
// once, so its Done{Seq: -1} may come before the Dones of Outs still in
// flight: count Dones, Seq numbers only the sent ones. A refusal is dropped
// only when 8 Dones are already owed and unread (an owner that reads
// nothing, flooded with Outs). Seq numbers the Outs sent successfully, from
// 1; it is -1 when the port refused the Out (too many in flight, bad data,
// nothing to send it on) or could not send it, and such an Out uses no
// number.
type Done struct {
	From actor.PID
	Seq  int
}

// Closed is a stream event: the port ended by itself (the peer closed or
// reset a connection, a timeout, the hardware failed). From is the port;
// Reason says why (the net package's reasons for tcp and tls). Normal (0)
// from tcp or tls means only that the peer finished sending: Outs still
// work until another Closed. After any other reason the port does nothing
// more: close it. A port sends at most one Normal and one terminal Closed.
type Closed struct {
	From   actor.PID
	Reason int
}

// Host configures a port that the program embedding the VM defines (embed
// mode, gogo_register_port in runtime/include/gogo.h): Arg and Data are
// passed to the host's open as they are. Such a port takes Post commands
// and sends its owner Post events.
type Host struct {
	Arg  int
	Data bytes.Bytes
}

// Post is a message from the program embedding the VM (embed mode). The
// host's gogo_post sends one to a registered name: Kind and Data are what
// the host gave, From is the zero PID; it arrives as an ordinary message
// under the receiver's mailbox quota (a post over it is dropped, and the
// receiver is not killed). Host-defined ports use it for their commands
// and events too; an event's From is the port (a command's From is
// ignored).
type Post struct {
	From actor.PID
	Kind int
	Data bytes.Bytes
}

// Open opens a port of the named driver with cfg; the caller becomes its
// owner. ok is false if the driver is unknown, cfg is invalid for it, the
// resource is taken, or memory runs out.
func Open(driver string, cfg any) (p actor.PID, ok bool) { panic("gogo only") }

// Close closes the port p. A stale or unknown p is ignored.
func Close(p actor.PID) { panic("gogo only") }

// Handoff makes the actor to the owner of port p: its later events go to
// to, to's exit closes it, and the memory charged for it (driver state,
// buffers the port holds) moves to to. Only p's owner may hand it off. ok
// is false (nothing changes) if the caller does not own p, p is not an open
// port, to is not a live actor, or to cannot take the charge within its
// memory limit. Handoff to the caller itself only resumes a paused port
// (e.g. an accepted net connection).
func Handoff(p actor.PID, to actor.PID) (ok bool) { panic("gogo only") }
